-- title:
--   Torus quotient tower over 𝔽ₚ of the finite part
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and a nonzero level $M$ with $p \mid M$ (`hpM`) and $p^2 \nmid M$ (`hpM2`), and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ containing every unit that becomes $1$ in $(\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ (`hHp`). Fix a valuation subring $P\ell$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $p$ a nonunit of $P\ell$ (`hPl`, the predicate `LiesOverPrime`), whose residue field has characteristic $p$ and is algebraically closed, and assume `hj`, that the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ lies in the field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`. The geometric data consist of: an integral model package $\mathfrak{X}$ of type [`ModularCurve.XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L81) for the two-chart models of $X_H$ over $R =$ [`ModularCurve.XHDRLevel.R p`](def/ModularCurve_XHDRModelAtP.html#L22) (the localisation of $\mathbb{Q}$ at $p$); level data $\Lambda$ and an object $O$ of type [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`](def/ModularCurve_JHNeronObjectAtP.html#L53), so that $O$ provides a scheme $O.G$ smooth, separated, surjective and of locally finite type over the base `base p` with connected fibres, a commutative relative group law $O.L$, an identification $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of $J_H(M) = \mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field of $X_H$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with the generic sections of $O.g$, Hecke sections $O.\mathrm{hecke}$, and a toric rank $O.\mathrm{toricRank}$. Two representability hypotheses are imposed: `hrep`, that the designation $(O.G, O.g, O.L.\mathrm{one})$ represents the subfunctor of the relative Picard functor of `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ cut out by fibrewise algebraic-equivalence-to-zero (`algEquivZeroCut`), and `hrepΛ`, the analogous statement for the level `ΓN p M H hpM` curve with section $\mathfrak{X}.\varepsilon_{\inf}$ followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\pi$ and the designation built from $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$, $\Lambda.L.\mathrm{one}$.
--
--   The arithmetic base is a Henselian local domain $R_h$, an $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra with injective structure map, such that every element of $R_h$ maps into $P\ell$ (`hRA`) and such that the maximal ideal of $R_h$ consists exactly of the elements whose image has $P\ell$-valuation $<1$ (`hRloc`); $R_h$ is moreover an algebra over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ for which an element maps to $0$ precisely when its image in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ has valuation $<1$ (`hres`), so that $R_h \to \mathbb{F}_p$ is the residue map.
--
--   The witness data are: a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{G}$ over $R_h$ of height $h$, that is, a tower of finite free cocommutative Hopf $R_h$-algebras $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with $\operatorname{rank} = p^{vh}$ and surjective transitions whose kernels are the $p^v$-torsion ideals; an additive map $\Delta$ from the points $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ to $J_H(M)$; and a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linear map $e$ between the Tate modules of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{Points}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}})$ and of $J_H(M)$. The hypotheses on $\Delta$ are: `hΔinj` ($\Delta$ injective); `hΔlev` (for every $v$, the subgroup $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^v)$ — generated by the $p^v$-torsion classes whose section extends to the place given by $\Lambda.\sigma_A$, the predicate `ExtendsToPlace` — is exactly the set of values of $\Delta$ on level-$v$ points); `hΔgal` (equivariance of $\Delta$ for any $\tau \in \mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and any $R_h$-algebra automorphism $\tau'$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ agreeing with $\tau$); and `hΔhecke` (for every set $S$ of naturals and every generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13), i.e. a $T_\ell$, $U_q$ or diamond symbol, there is a family of coalgebra $R_h$-algebra endomorphisms $\varphi_v$ of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ commuting with the transitions such that $\Delta$ carries precomposition by $\varphi_v$ to the operator `genOpH M H S g` on $J_H(M)$). The hypotheses on $e$ are: `he` ($e$ acts componentwise by $\Delta$), `heinj` ($e$ injective), `herange` (a Tate vector lies in the range of $e$ exactly when its $n$-th component lies in $O.\mathrm{finPts}(p^n)$ for every $n$), `hegal` ($e$ intertwines $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{tateModuleRep}$ with `JH.tateGaloisRep` in the same sense as `hΔgal`), `hsat` (the range of $e$ is saturated: $p y$ in the range forces $y$ in the range), and `hcoker` (the cokernel of $e$ is $\mathbb{Z}_p$-linearly isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}_p^{O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$). In addition `htor` requires every element of $O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$ to be a value of $\Delta$ on a level-$v$ point.
--
--   The toric sub-object is given by a $p$-divisible group $\mathcal{B}$ over $R_h$ of height $h_B$ together with a family $\psi_v : \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ of coalgebra $R_h$-algebra maps, a natural number $h'$, and the numerical hypotheses `hhB`, $h = O.\mathrm{toricRank} + h_B$, and `hhB2`, $h_B = 2h'$. The hypotheses on $\psi$ are: `hψt` (compatibility with the transitions); `hψker` (for a level-$v$ point $x$ of $\mathcal{G}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the point obtained by composing with $\psi_v$ is trivial precisely when $\Delta(x) \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}(p^v)$); `hψsurj` (every level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{B}$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ arises by composing some level-$v$ point of $\mathcal{G}$ with $\psi_v$); `hψred` (if the $\psi_v$-image of $x$ is congruent to the counit modulo the maximal ideal of $P\ell$, valuationwise on all of $\mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, then so is $x$ on all of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$); and `hperiod` (for every $v$, every $\sigma$ in the inertia subgroup of $P\ell$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, every $p^v$-torsion class $z$ of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of the function field of $X_H$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and every level-$v$ point $y$ with $\Delta(y) = \sigma z - z$, the $\psi_v$-image of $y$ is congruent to the counit in the above sense).
--
--   Finally the tower $\mathcal{G}$ is realised inside $O.G$: a ring map $\rho_h : R \to R_h$ and morphisms $\iota_v : \operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v) \to O.G$ are given, subject to: `hρh` (compatibility of $\rho_h$ with the maps to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$); `hιbase` ($\iota_v$ followed by $O.g$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $R_h \to \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec} \rho_h$); `hιcl` (the induced map to the pullback of $O.g$ along $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ is a closed immersion); `hιp` ($\iota_v$ followed by multiplication by $p^v$ for $O.L$ factors through the unit section); `hιpts` (for a level-$v$ point $x$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the section $O.\mathrm{pts}(\Delta(x))$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $x$ followed by $\iota_v$); `hιmul` ($\iota_v$ is multiplicative: for points $x, y$ of $\mathcal{G}$ at level $v$ with values in any $R_h$-algebra $B$, lying over the base as required by the two side hypotheses, the section attached to $xy$ is the $O.L$-product of those attached to $x$ and $y$); `hιt` (compatibility with the transitions); `hιhecke` (for every Hecke generator $g$ as above there is a compatible family $\varphi_v$ of coalgebra endomorphisms of $\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ with $\operatorname{Spec}\varphi_v$ followed by $\iota_v$ equal to $\iota_v$ followed by $O.\mathrm{hecke}\,S\,g$, and with $\Delta$ intertwining $\varphi_v$ and `genOpH M H S g`); and `hιfin` (for every $v$ the canonical map $j_v$ from $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$ to the base change along $\operatorname{Spec}\rho_h$ of the $p^v$-kernel of $O.L$ — the pullback of $p^v$-multiplication against the unit section — is simultaneously an open immersion and a closed immersion, and every point of that base change lying over the closed point of $R_h$ belongs to the image of $j_v$).
--
--   One further hypothesis, `hjB`, requires that for every $v$ the base change $\mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \psi_v$, formed as `Bialgebra.TensorProduct.map` of the identity of $\mathbb{Z}/p$ with $\psi_v$, be injective.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a family of types $T_v$ ($v \in \mathbb{N}$), each carrying a commutative ring structure and a Hopf algebra structure over $\mathbb{Z}/p$ which is cocommutative, finite and free as a $\mathbb{Z}/p$-module, together with coalgebra $\mathbb{Z}/p$-algebra maps $t^T_v : T_{v+1} \to T_v$ and $\pi^T_v : \mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v \to T_v$, such that all of the following hold:
--
--   (i) each $t^T_v$ is surjective;
--
--   (ii) $\dim_{\mathbb{F}_p} T_v = p^{\,v \cdot O.\mathrm{toricRank}}$ for every $v$;
--
--   (iii) $\ker t^T_v$ is the $p^v$-torsion ideal of $T_{v+1}$, i.e. [`PDivisibleGroup.Hopf.torsionIdeal (ZMod p) (T (v+1)) (p ^ v)`](def/PDivisibleGroup_Basic.html#L157), the ideal generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^v$;
--
--   (iv) each $\pi^T_v$ is surjective;
--
--   (v) the base-changed transition $\mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{transition}\,v$ followed by $\pi^T_v$ equals $\pi^T_{v+1}$ followed by $t^T_v$;
--
--   (vi) for every $v$, the kernel of $\pi^T_v$ as a ring map is the ideal of $\mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v$ generated by the image, under $\mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \psi_v$, of the kernel of the counit of $\mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{B}.\mathrm{level}\,v$, that is $\ker \pi^T_v = (\mathbb{F}_p \otimes \psi_v)(\mathrm{Aug}) \cdot (\mathbb{F}_p \otimes_{R_h} \mathcal{G}.\mathrm{level}\,v)$.
--
--   The conclusion asserts nothing about Verschiebung or Cartier duality on the tower $T$.
--
--   This is the characteristic-$p$ half of the Raynaud-quotient step in the local analysis at $p$ (with $p \| M$) of the Néron object attached to $J_H(M)$: the quotient of the special fibre of the finite part by the toric $p$-divisible subgroup $\mathcal{B}$ is exhibited as a $p$-divisible tower of height the toric rank, presented by generators and relations over $\mathbb{F}_p$. It feeds [`ModularCurve.exists_torusQuotient_multiplicative_exact_raynaudQuotient_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_torusQuotient_multiplicative_exact_raynaudQuotient_finPts_jHNeronObjectAtP_of_finPtsWitness), where the multiplicative structure of the tower is added.
